========================================================================
LEGEND
========================================================================
( [T0] -- single-object symbols were already assigned above with the  )
( "Name Char" shortcut. This section is where COMPOSITE symbols live: )
( "or" properties (see [T5]) and "and" stacked-object combos (see     )
( [T5]/[T7]) that need a character of their own so they can be placed )
( directly into a level grid.                                        )

Fire = Spark or Blaze
x = Spark and Wood
y = Coin and Glow
Ring = Ring3 or Ring2 or Ring1 or Ring0

========================================================================
SOUNDS
========================================================================
( [T0] -- intentionally empty for this tutorial. )

========================================================================
COLLISIONLAYERS
========================================================================
( [T0] -- order here also sets draw order, bottom to top.             )
( [T7] -- everything listed on the SAME line below can never share a  )
( cell with anything else on that line. Wall, Player, Crate, Smart-    )
( Crate, and Wood all share Solid on purpose - that's what makes       )
( them block/replace each other naturally. Bell needs its OWN layer,   )
( separate from Player's - otherwise the two could never overlap, and  )
( the [T9] rule below (which requires exactly that overlap) would be   )
( impossible to ever trigger.                                          )

Background
Glow
Coin
Bell, RungBell, Exit
Wall, Player, Crate, SmartCrate, Wood
Spark, Blaze, Flame, Embers, Ash
Flood, Ping, Pulse, Ring3, Ring2, Ring1, Ring0

========================================================================
RULES
========================================================================

( [T1] one pass, top to bottom --------------------------------------- )
( [T3] rule GROUPS: + and STARTLOOP/ENDLOOP --------------------------- )
( A turn runs every rule below ONCE, in order, top to bottom - not the )
( whole file as one big loop, unless you ask for that explicitly.      )
( Compare these two nearly-identical push mechanics: )

[ > Crate | Crate ] -> [ > Crate | > Crate ]
[ > Player | Crate ] -> [ > Player | > Crate ]

( Plain Crate: the "keep pushing" rule (line 1) already ran to         )
( exhaustion and finished before the "start pushing" rule (line 2)     )
( ever marks anything as moving. So with two Crates in a row, line 1   )
( found nothing to do, line 2 marks only the front Crate, and that     )
( Crate can't move because the second one's still in the way. Net      )
( result: nothing moves at all. )

[ > SmartCrate | SmartCrate ] -> [ > SmartCrate | > SmartCrate ]
+ [ > Player | SmartCrate ] -> [ > Player | > SmartCrate ]

( SmartCrate: the leading + glues these two rules into one GROUP,      )
( which the engine re-tries as a pair, back and forth, until nothing   )
( changes. Now "start pushing" marks the first crate moving, the       )
( group loops back, "keep pushing" notices and marks the second one    )
( too, loops back again, and so on - a proper domino. STARTLOOP /      )
( ENDLOOP does the same job for bigger blocks that all need to feed    )
( each other. -- next: [T2] )

( [T2] a single rule already loops itself ------------------------------ )
( You don't always need + or STARTLOOP. Each rule on its own is        )
( already "applied as often as it can be" before the engine moves to   )
( the next line. Watch this fill an entire room from one keypress: )

[ Flood | no Flood no Wall ] -> [ Flood | Flood ]

( No loop construct anywhere above, and it doesn't need one - but      )
( self-looping only cascades a rule against ITSELF. It won't pull a    )
( DIFFERENT rule listed below it back into the loop; that's what +     )
( and STARTLOOP are actually for (see [T1]/[T3] above). -- next: [T4] )

( [T4] direction keywords, and the horizontal/vertical shortcut ------- )
( No direction keyword at all = checked in all four compass directions )
( automatically. Ping is deliberately restricted to two of them; Pulse )
( is left wide open: )

horizontal [ Ping | no Ping no Wall ] -> [ Ping | Ping ]
[ Pulse | no Pulse no Wall ] -> [ Pulse | Pulse ]

( "horizontal" is shorthand for writing out separate LEFT and RIGHT    )
( lines (there's a matching "vertical" too). Ping only ever spreads    )
( into a straight horizontal line. Pulse spreads into a plus/diamond,  )
( because leaving the direction off checks LEFT, RIGHT, UP, and DOWN   )
( all at once. -- next: [T5] )

( [T5] "or" properties vs "and" aggregates ------------------------------ )
( [T6] only things REFERRED to, then dropped, get removed -------------- )
( Fire = Spark or Blaze isn't a real object - it's a label meaning     )
( "whichever of these is actually here." Neither Spark nor Blaze ever  )
( stops being itself just because a rule mentions "Fire." )

[ Fire Wood ] -> [ Fire ]
[ Fire | Wood ] -> [ Fire | Blaze ]

( Line 1: the cell has Fire (Spark or Blaze - a different layer than   )
( Wood, so they're allowed to share a cell) AND Wood. "Fire" is        )
( repeated on the right in the same slot it matched on the left, so    )
( whichever object it actually was is left completely untouched. Wood  )
( is mentioned on the left but dropped on the right - so it's          )
( removed. That's [T6]: only referenced-then-omitted things vanish;    )
( things never mentioned at all are never touched.                     )
( Line 2: adjacent Wood catches Blaze. And since Blaze counts as       )
( Fire, and a single rule self-loops against itself (remember [T2]?),  )
( a whole row of Wood ignites in ONE keypress, not one tile per turn.  )
( -- next: [T7] )

( [T7] collision layers: what can/can't share a cell -------------------- )
( Coin (Pickup layer) and Glow (Floor layer) sit on different layers,  )
( so they CAN stack - that's what made "y = Coin and Glow" legal up in )
( the LEGEND. Compare the CollisionLayers block above: everything      )
( grouped on the Solid line can never do that with each other. )

[ Player Coin ] -> [ Player ]

( Coin is referred to and dropped -> removed. Glow was never           )
( mentioned in this rule at all -> left alone. Walk onto the tile      )
( below and watch the coin disappear while the glow stays put.         )
( -- next: [T8] )

( [T8] again: self-driving multi-turn animation ------------------------- )
( again schedules one more turn automatically, with a short pause,     )
( and keeps doing that for as long as something is still changing.     )
( Without it, this decay chain would need one fresh keypress per       )
( stage instead of running on its own: )

[ Flame ] -> [ Embers ] again
[ Embers ] -> [ Ash ] again

( One keypress starts it; the rest plays by itself. This is the       )
( trick behind any multi-turn spreading/decaying effect - explosions,  )
( fuses, dying fires - stage the states, put again on the              )
( transitions, press go once. -- next: [T9] )

( [T9] late: rules that run after movement resolves --------------------- )
( Ordinary rules run BEFORE this turn's movement is actually applied - )
( they see where things are ABOUT to go, not where they end up. late   )
( rules run afterward, once positions are final. )

late [ Player Bell ] -> [ Player RungBell ]

( Without late, this would only catch the ring one turn AFTER you'd    )
( already arrived (the official docs hit this exact issue with a      )
( crate-vanishing example). With late, arriving and ringing happen on  )
( the same turn. -- next: [T10] )

( [T10] grab bag --------------------------------------------------------- )
( - DEBUG: add a line that just says "debug" up in the Prelude, near   )
(   "title" and "author," and reload. It shows the exact compiled      )
(   rules, including how "or" properties and no-direction rules        )
(   actually expand under the hood.                                    )
( - NO keyword: you've been reading it everywhere above ("no Wall",    )
(   "no Flood") - it means "this must NOT be present," and it works    )
(   even for objects that were never mentioned on the left otherwise.  )
( - Object identity: PuzzleScript never promises the Flame you see     )
(   after a rule fires is "the same" Flame that was there a moment     )
(   before - a replacement and a destroy-then-recreate look identical  )
(   to the engine. Don't try to track one specific tile's identity     )
(   across a rule; build state machines out of distinct objects        )
(   instead, the way [T8] does with Flame/Embers/Ash. -- next: [T11] )

( [T11] capping how far a self-looping spread goes --------------------- )
( PuzzleScript has no per-cell counter, so "spread outward, but stop    )
( after N steps" has to be spelled out as N distinct object identities  )
( - one per step remaining - exactly like Wood -> Spark -> Flame, or    )
( Explosion:C -> Relay:B -> Relay:A. Here's that same idea, generic     )
( and reusable: )

[ Ring3 | no Ring no Wall ] -> [ Ring3 | Ring2 ]
[ Ring2 | no Ring no Wall ] -> [ Ring2 | Ring1 ]
[ Ring1 | no Ring no Wall ] -> [ Ring1 | Ring0 ]

( Ring0 has no rule of its own - nothing ever spreads FROM it. That     )
( missing fourth rule IS the cap: a single Ring3 seed produces exactly  )
( a radius-3 diamond and then stops dead, no matter how much open       )
( floor surrounds it - unlike Pulse back in [T4], which just kept       )
( going until it hit a wall. Rename and recolor the four stages and     )
( you've got a fixed-radius Pulse, or a fixed-radius blast for your     )
( real project. Want it to grow one ring per turn instead of resolving  )
( instantly? Put "again" on each transition, same as [T8] did for      )
( Flame/Embers/Ash. )

========================================================================
WINCONDITIONS
========================================================================
( [T0] -- level_select / enable_level_select is a PuzzleScript PLUS      )
( feature (a third-party fork), not part of vanilla PuzzleScript - it   )
( won't compile here. Instead, every level below has a green Exit tile )
( (d) sitting somewhere easy to reach right from the start. Walk onto  )
( it whenever you're done poking at that demo - winning auto-advances  )
( to the next level (and its MESSAGE), same as any PuzzleScript game.  )

Some Player on Exit

========================================================================
LEVELS
========================================================================

( [T1] + [T3] -- push the Crates (top corridor) vs the SmartCrates    )
( (bottom corridor). Same shove, different result. The middle row is  )
( just a connector so the green Exit (d) is reachable either way. )
##############
#P.CC........#
#......d.....#
#P.SS........#
##############
MESSAGE [T1]/[T3]: the Crate row didn't budge, because the push rules never got grouped, so the domino effect never happens. SmartCrate pushed as one unit because of the leading +.

( [T2] -- one Flood seed, one keypress. Watch it claim the whole room. )
#############
#o........dP#
#############
MESSAGE [T2]: no + or STARTLOOP anywhere in that rule, and the whole room still filled in a single turn - a lone rule already loops itself to exhaustion.

( [T4] -- Ping (i) is direction-restricted, Pulse (u) isn't. Move around )
( a bit and compare the shapes each one grows into. )
###############
#......P......#
#d............#
#......i......#
#.............#
###############
#......u......#
#.............#
###############
MESSAGE [T4]: Ping only ever grows into a flat horizontal line. Pulse grew a plus/diamond, because leaving the direction off a rule checks all four compass directions at once.

( [T5] + [T6] -- x is Spark-and-Wood stacked in one cell (walk near it, )
( press a direction, watch the Wood disappear from under the Spark).   )
( z is a lit Blaze sitting next to a run of Wood (w) - watch the whole  )
( row catch in one keypress. )
#############
#P..x......d#
#############
#zwwwww.....#
#############
MESSAGE [T5]/[T6]: the spark-on-wood tile lost its Wood but kept the Spark - Wood was referenced then dropped, so it was removed; Spark was referenced and repeated, so it stayed. The whole row of Wood caught at once, same as the Flood did in [T2].

( [T7] -- y is a Coin stacked on a Glow. Walk onto it. )
#########
#P..y..d#
#########
MESSAGE [T7]: the coin vanished, the glow stayed - two different layers, two independent objects, one rule that only mentions Coin.

( [T8] -- a lit Flame (f) sitting by itself. Press any direction once )
( and then just watch. )
#########
#P..f..d#
#########
MESSAGE [T8]: one keypress, and Flame, Embers, then Ash played out on their own over the next couple of automatic turns, thanks to again.

( [T9] -- walk onto the Bell (b) and see how fast it rings. )
#########
#P..b..d#
#########
MESSAGE [T9]: the bell rang the instant you arrived, because the ringing rule was marked late and checked your FINAL position for this turn, not your position before you moved.

( [T11] -- Ring3 (r) sits dead center. The room is wide open on every )
( side, well past a radius of 3 - watch it stop anyway. )
###########
#P.......d#
#.........#
#.........#
#.........#
#....r....#
#.........#
#.........#
#.........#
#.........#
###########
MESSAGE [T11]: a radius-3 diamond, then nothing - even though open floor keeps going for another six tiles in every direction. Ring0 has no rule of its own, so the chain has nowhere left to spread to.
MESSAGE That's the whole map. Scroll back up to [T10] for the grab bag, or go pull these techniques into your real project.
